                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "66339efa-0d30-41c1-9ca7-f56cc790c271",
                        "source_entry_id": "623b2abd-b42a-31f1-a6b5-9645722328a2",
                        "source_sense_id": "3cb1a6bb-ce6a-3d23-ba03-e4f3ed852593",
                        "source_sense_description": "ein Disaccharid",
                        "target_lang_word": "trehalose",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "trehalose",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "39a7a654-51c1-3925-af8a-0e9943d0a8a6",
                                        "entry_id": "07c71e1a-c355-39f0-ac4a-da477eced0dc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A disaccharide formed from two glucose units; it is an isomer of maltose."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "866b457f-5891-4c45-923a-995bbc2f4c45",
                                                "sense_id": "39a7a654-51c1-3925-af8a-0e9943d0a8a6",
                                                "text": "Major components of the polar lipids are the acylated trehaloses which differ considerably in their acylation patterns to trehalose and in fatty acid content."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "41b10109-91ac-4749-b3d7-eef5d856011a",
                                                "sense_id": "39a7a654-51c1-3925-af8a-0e9943d0a8a6",
                                                "text": "The most important of these is the common sugar furnished by the sugar cane, hence termed cane sugar, related to which are some others of small importance, viz., trehalose, melezitose, and melitose, represented by the general formula (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁,xH₂O)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "394134d0-5691-4bee-b4ad-b6e327987eef",
                                                "sense_id": "39a7a654-51c1-3925-af8a-0e9943d0a8a6",
                                                "text": "The researchers fed a natural sugar called trehalose to mice genetically engineered to have a severe version of Huntington's disease."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5b58d2db-34bd-4533-a066-7e2f40e0335a",
                                                "sense_id": "39a7a654-51c1-3925-af8a-0e9943d0a8a6",
                                                "text": "Two bacterial strains that have plagued hospitals around the country may have been at least partly fueled by a sugar additive in our food products, scientists say. Trehalose, a sugar that is added to a wide range of food products, could have allowed certain strains of Clostridium difficile to become far more virulent than they were before, a new study finds."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d1d86ea0-ff99-45e4-a487-f92c3d2393aa",
                        "source_entry_id": "623b2abd-b42a-31f1-a6b5-9645722328a2",
                        "source_sense_id": "3cb1a6bb-ce6a-3d23-ba03-e4f3ed852593",
                        "source_sense_description": "ein Disaccharid",
                        "target_lang_word": "tréhalose",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "tréhalose",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4023c6f6-9299-33e1-8a2e-c41afbb1110e",
                                        "entry_id": "5a152c4a-e5ac-3551-9fd9-b8a661ac6176",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Diholoside cristallisable qui constitue une partie du tréhala."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d9d358bc-12d0-4325-b27a-83dea974ddda",
                                                "sense_id": "4023c6f6-9299-33e1-8a2e-c41afbb1110e",
                                                "text": "Jusqu’ici, on pensait qu’en l’absence d’eau, les tardigrades survivaient grâce à un sucre, le tréhalose. Ce dernier est essentiel à de nombreux organismes comme les levures, les artémies (petits crustacés) ou certains nématodes pour résister à la dessiccation. … Mais à l’heure actuelle, la présence de tréhalose chez les tardigrades n’est cependant pas confirmée ; …"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "05d5ed3b-1389-4867-9feb-dfd00b8a68d1",
                                                "sense_id": "4023c6f6-9299-33e1-8a2e-c41afbb1110e",
                                                "text": "Leurs tissus se déshydratent et l’eau intracellulaire est remplacée par des protéines spécifiques (protéines de choc thermique) et du tréhalose, un sucre non réducteur très visqueux qui permet de conserver un volume intracellulaire minimum tout en limitant les agrégats de biomolécules."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
